# What should Black play against 1.e4?
teaches: ["openings/black-against-e4"]
voices: {"a":"gemini:Charon | calm, warm chess coach","b":"gemini:Puck | eager club player, relaxed and good-humoured; never exaggerated"}
prompt: "How does 1…e5 meet the Italian with …Bc5? What do you do against the Scotch and other early d4 moves?"
next: {"title":"What should Black play against 1.d4?","href":"https://www.drawcast.app/#gh=hmelberg/drawcast-library/chess-30-days/17-what-should-black-play-against-1-d4.cast"}

## Warm-up: double attacks
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1bqk2r/pppp1ppp/1bn5/4P3/2B1n3/2P2N2/PP3PPP/RNBQK2R w KQkq - 0 7, moves: [Qd5, Bxf2+, Kf1, O-O, Qxe4]}
      - {fen: r1b1k3/p2n1ppp/1pp5/1N6/8/4B3/PPP2PPP/3R2K1 w - - 0 1, moves: [Nc7+, Kd8, Nxa8]}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0

    card title Warm-up

A: Today: what Black plays against 1.e4. First, two warm-ups from last week.
    draw position

A: An Italian. Black pulled the bishop back to b6, then the knight grabbed the pawn on e4.
    highlight piece_b6 piece_e4

B: The knight looks safe. Nothing of White's attacks it.
    highlight piece_e4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line 1 hints ["One move, two threats.","Your queen, and the square f7."] themes ["opening","fork"] voice a teaches ["openings/black-against-e4","tactics/fork"]
        right: Queen to d5: it threatens mate on f7 with the bishop, and it hits the knight on e4 at the same time.
        wrong: Which queen move threatens mate and hits the knight?

A: Black's best try is a check on f2.
    animate plies_shown 2 duration 1.3

B: The king steps aside,
    animate plies_shown 3 duration 1.3

B: and Black castles out of the mate.
    animate plies_shown 4 duration 1.3

A: But the knight on e4 falls: a piece for a pawn.
    animate plies_shown 5 duration 1.3

A: Second one. White's knight on b5 is attacked by a pawn.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: Material's level. I'd pull my knight back to safety.
    highlight piece_b5 piece_c6

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A knight check.","Which square touches the king and the rook?"] themes ["fork"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork"]
        right: Knight to c7, check, instead of retreating: the king steps to d8, and the knight takes the rook on a8.
        wrong: Before it retreats, look for a check that hits something else too.

A: Both times, one move made two threats. Today Black answers 1.e4 the same way: hitting something with almost every move.
    highlight piece_a8

## Hit back: the Italian and the Scotch
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Bc5, c3, Nf6, d4, exd4, e5, d5, Bb5, Ne4], flip: true}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, d4, exd4, Nxd4, Bc5, Nc3, Bxd4], flip: true, arrows: [{from: c5, to: d4}, {from: c6, to: d4}]}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, d4, exd4, Qxd4, Nc6, Qe3, Nf6], flip: true}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Bc5, c3, Nf6, d4, exd4, cxd4, Bb4+], flip: true}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 5
sources:
    - id: giuoco
      title: Giuoco Piano
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: Giuoco Piano is Italian for 'quiet game' (3...Bc5, ECO C50–C54). After 4.c3 Nf6 5.d4 exd4, 6.cxd4 Bb4+ is the classical line; against 6.e5 Black's best reply is 6...d5 (7.Bb5 Ne4); after 5.O-O the pawn can be taken with 5...Nxe4.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giuoco_Piano
    - id: scotch
      title: Scotch Game
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: Named after an 1824 correspondence match between Edinburgh and London; in the Classical Variation 4...Bc5 the bishop attacks the knight on d4, and 5.Be3, 5.Nxc6 or 5.Nb3 deal with it.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scotch_Game
    - id: centre
      title: Center Game
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: After 1.e4 e5 2.d4 exd4 3.Qxd4, Black's usual 3...Nc6 develops with a gain of tempo; 4.Qe3 (the Paulsen Attack) is considered the best queen move, and Black usually answers 4...Nf6.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Center_Game

    card title "Hit back"

A: Today we sit on Black's side. Against the Italian, White's bishop comes to c4, aiming at f7.
    draw position

B: I copy it. Bishop to c5, aiming at f2.
    animate plies_shown 6 duration 1.3

A: Good: the Giuoco Piano, the quiet game. Now White plays c3, to push d4 and hit your bishop.
    blocking false

    highlight piece_c5 duration 2

    animate plies_shown 7 duration 1.3

B: Then I hold the centre first. Pawn to d6.
    move piece_d7 to.ref sq_d6 ghost true duration 1

    move piece_d7 to.ref sq_d7 duration 0.6

    erase piece_d7_ghost

A: Pawn to d6 is solid. But knight to f6 is sharper: it hits e4 right now.
    animate plies_shown 8 duration 1.3

A: And c3 took the knight's square, so nothing guards e4. If White castles, you take it.
    highlight piece_e4

A: So White pushes d4.
    animate plies_shown 9 duration 1.3

B: And I take.
    animate plies_shown 10 duration 1.3

    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 11 duration 0.4

A: If White takes back with the pawn, bishop to b4 comes with check.
    animate plies_shown 12 duration 1.3

A: The sharper try is pawn to e5, kicking your knight. Your best answer?
    animate position_shown 0 plies_shown 11 duration 0.4

    quiz "Your knight is attacked. Your best answer?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 935 buttons_at.y 400 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: Pawn to d5: it hits the bishop, a threat answered with a threat.
        wrong: Look for a move that attacks something of White's.
        * Ng8
        + d5
        * dxc3

    animate plies_shown 12 duration 1.3

A: The Scotch, named after an 1824 match between Edinburgh and London: d4 on move three.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 5 duration 0.4

B: I take again.
    animate plies_shown 6 duration 1.3

A: And the knight takes back.
    animate plies_shown 7 duration 1.3

A: Bishop to c5 hits that knight, and so does your knight on c6.
    animate plies_shown 8 duration 1.3

    draw arrow_0 arrow_1 parallel true

A: But say White just develops: knight to c3. What does Black win?
    animate plies_shown 9 duration 1.3

    quiz "White played Nc3. What does Black win?" on_canvas true buttons_at.x 935 buttons_at.y 400 buttons_layout column voice a
        right: The d4 knight: two attackers, your bishop and knight, and one defender, the queen. Either piece can take.
        wrong: Count the attackers and defenders on d4.
        * Nothing yet
        + The d4 knight
        * The e4 pawn

    erase arrow_0 arrow_1

A: Here the bishop takes, and the knight is yours.
    animate plies_shown 10 duration 1.3

A: The Centre Game pushes d4 on move two, and you take at once.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 4 duration 0.4

B: Then the queen takes back. Big queen in the middle!
    animate plies_shown 5 duration 1.3

A: Knight to c6 hits her, and she moves again: a move gained for you.
    animate plies_shown 6 duration 1.3

A: Usually she goes to e3,
    animate plies_shown 7 duration 1.3

A: and knight to f6 hits e4.
    animate plies_shown 8 duration 1.3

A: So almost every move you play hits something, and White has little time for free moves.
    highlight piece_f6 piece_e4

## Your turn: Black hits back
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Four for four. White should think twice before 1.e4.
    good: Most of them. The rest will turn up in your games.
    poor: Look for what each move can hit, and try again.
    none: The puzzles will wait for you.
use: chess_board
with:
    positions:
      - {fen: r1b1k1nr/pppp1ppp/2N2q2/2b5/4P3/3B4/PPP2PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 2 6, moves: [Qxf2#], flip: true}
      - {fen: r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/8/4P3/2Q5/PPP2PPP/RNB1KBNR b KQkq - 2 4, moves: [Bb4], flip: true}
      - {fen: r1bqk2r/pppp1ppp/5n2/2b1n3/2BPP3/2P5/PP3PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 0 6, moves: [Nxc4, dxc5], flip: true}
      - {fen: r2q1rk1/ppp2ppp/3p4/8/2BnP1b1/2P3P1/PP1Q1P1P/RN3RK1 b - - 0 1, moves: [Nf3+, Kg2, Nxd2], flip: true}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Bc5], flip: true}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Bc5, c3, Nf6], flip: true}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, d4, exd4, Nxd4, Bc5], flip: true}
      - {fen: rnbqkbnr/pppppppp/8/8/8/8/PPPPPPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq - 0 1, moves: [e4, e5, d4, exd4, Qxd4, Nc6, Qe3, Nf6], flip: true}
    position_shown: 0
    plies_shown: 0
    openings:
      - {name: Giuoco Piano, eco: C50, side: black, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Bc5], idea: 'Your bishop aims at f2, just as White''s aims at f7.'}
      - {name: Giuoco Piano with c3, eco: C53, side: black, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, Bc4, Bc5, c3, Nf6], idea: 'The knight hits e4 at once, and c3 has taken its defender''s square.'}
      - {name: Scotch Game, eco: C45, side: black, moves: [e4, e5, Nf3, Nc6, d4, exd4, Nxd4, Bc5], idea: 'Take the pawn, then hit the knight on d4 a second time.'}
      - {name: Centre Game, eco: C22, side: black, moves: [e4, e5, d4, exd4, Qxd4, Nc6, Qe3, Nf6], idea: 'The knight hits the queen, and when she goes to e3, the other knight hits e4.'}
sources:
    - id: scotch
      title: Scotch Game
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: After 4...Bc5 5.Nxc6, Black's usual 5...Qf6 threatens ...Qxf2 mate, so White must stop it.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scotch_Game
    - id: centre
      title: Center Game
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: After 1.e4 e5 2.d4 exd4 3.Qxd4, 3...Nc6 develops with a gain of tempo on the queen.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Center_Game
    - id: giuoco
      title: Giuoco Piano
      authors: Wikipedia contributors
      year: 2026
      finding: The Giuoco Piano is 1.e4 e5 2.Nf3 Nc6 3.Bc4 Bc5; after 4.c3 Black usually answers 4...Nf6.
      url: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giuoco_Piano

    card title "Your turn"

A: Now that Black's moves hit back, your turn. Four puzzles, all from Black's side.
    draw position

A: The Scotch. White took your knight on c6, your queen went to f6, and White just developed the bishop.
    highlight piece_f6 piece_d3

    pause 0.3

    ask "Mate in one." widget chess line true hints ["Checks first.","What guards f2?"] themes ["opening","mating pattern"] voice a teaches ["openings/black-against-e4","tactics/mating-patterns"]
        right: Queen takes f2, mate. The bishop on c5 guards her, and the king has no square.
        wrong: Which square next to White's king does only the king defend?

B: So queen to f6 came with a threat.
    highlight piece_c5

A: The Centre Game. White's queen ran from your knight to c3.
    animate position_shown 1 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["What stands behind the queen?","A bishop move."] themes ["opening","pin"] voice a teaches ["openings/black-against-e4","tactics/pin"]
        right: Bishop to b4 pins the queen to the king: she falls for a bishop.
        wrong: Look along the diagonal from the queen to White's king.

B: She can't leave the line. And queen takes b4 loses her to the knight.
    highlight piece_e1

A: The Italian with c3. White took on e5, and d4 forks your bishop and knight.
    animate position_shown 2 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

B: I save the bishop.
    highlight piece_c5

    pause 0.3

    ask "Keep your extra piece." widget chess line true hints ["Captures first.","Your knight on e5 can take something."] themes ["opening","in-between move"] voice a teaches ["openings/black-against-e4","calculation/in-between-move"]
        right: Knight takes c4 first, instead of saving the bishop. White takes your bishop, and you stay a knight up for a pawn.
        wrong: Before you save anything, what can your attacked knight capture?

A: One from week two. White's pawn on c3 kicks your knight.
    animate position_shown 3 plies_shown 0 duration 0.4

    pause 0.3

    ask "Win material." widget chess line true hints ["A knight check.","Which square touches the king and the queen?"] themes ["fork"] voice a teaches ["tactics/fork"]
        right: Knight to f3, check: the king steps aside, and the queen on d2 falls.
        wrong: Before it retreats, look for a check that hits something else too.

A: Now the lines themselves: the Giuoco Piano,
    animate position_shown 4 plies_shown 6 duration 0.4

A: against c3,
    animate position_shown 5 plies_shown 8 duration 0.4

A: the Scotch,
    animate position_shown 6 plies_shown 8 duration 0.4

A: and the Centre Game.
    animate position_shown 7 plies_shown 8 duration 0.4

A: Play them from memory, as Black.
    explore activity openings_drill

A: Almost every Black move today came with a threat. That's how you take White's time away.
    highlight piece_c6 piece_f6

    erase position

A: Homework: one slow game today, fifteen plus ten or slower.
    text hw "15 + 10" x 500 y 470 font_size 110

A: Then find its first mistake.
    text hw2 "1st mistake?" x 500 y 320 font_size 84

